// chirp.strata -- timeline-fanout litmus (phase 2 exit criterion, T-0072).
//
// Surface-syntax twin of the phase-2 capacity feature set
// (docs/strata/roadmap.md#the-litmus-program). Stresses the
// "averages-lie" golden: identical aggregate demand proves under a
// mean-based ceiling and refutes under a zipf-skewed hottest-shard
// ceiling, plus a growth-horizon claim that flips a passing utilization
// to REFUTED once compound growth crosses the 24-month saturation
// horizon.
//
// GAP (reported, not worked around): the `store` surface construct parses
// a `capacity` prop (surface.md#std-infra), but `_infra.py::elaborate_infra`
// hardcodes `capacity=None` when desugaring a store into a `Node`
// (src/frob/strata/_infra.py:101), so a UTILIZATION claim against a store
// always refutes "declares no capacity" regardless of the declared
// capacity block. `_infra.py` is outside this ticket's scope
// (docs/strata/**, tickets.md, strata-core/**, Makefile, .github/**,
// design/litmus/**, tests/**), so this model routes capacity-bearing
// shards through plain `node`s fed by the `tweets` store instead of
// putting capacity directly on the store -- the store itself stays the
// source of truth and the cache source, exactly as std.infra intends,
// it just cannot ALSO carry a capacity claim target until that gap is
// fixed. See the Done report for the filed-gap note.
//
// tested end to end (parse -> elaborate -> evaluate_claims) by
// tests/unit/strata/test_litmus_chirp.py; these are permanent CI goldens.

module chirp

node author : foreign { clearance Public; }
node api : trusted { clearance Internal; }

// Source of truth for tweets (also the cache's source below). No capacity
// block here -- see the GAP note above for why a capacity claim can't
// target the store directly yet.
store tweets : trusted { clearance Internal; }

// The hot shard: fed from tweets via a `fanout 5` write-amplification
// path (each tweet fans out to followers' shards), giving 150 req/s of
// propagated demand -- tweets' own ingest demand (30 req/s, propagated
// from f_ingest through f_tweet_write) times fanout 5. `skew zipf 1.5`
// marks it as the sharded, hot-rank-1 resource, so the utilization claim
// below checks the HOTTEST shard, not the mean.
node tweets_hot : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    capacity 100 req/s replicas 1..4;
    skew zipf 1.5;
}

// The averages-lie twin: byte-for-byte the same capacity and the same
// propagated demand (same source, same fanout), but with no `skew` attr,
// so it falls back to the plain mean-based ceiling check.
node tweets_mean : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    capacity 100 req/s replicas 1..4;
}

// A third shard, sized so TODAY's utilization passes comfortably, but
// `growth 10 %` compounds monthly demand growth on its write path fast
// enough to cross the 80% limit inside the 24-month saturation horizon --
// flipping what would otherwise be a clean PROVED into REFUTED with a
// "saturates in N months" detail.
node tweets_growth : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    capacity 100 req/s replicas 1..1;
}

// The materialized timeline: a cache of the sharded store, invalidated by
// the write that lands new tweets in the store.
cache timeline of tweets {
    ttl 30 s;
    hit 90 %;
    invalidate_on f_tweet_write;
}

flow f_ingest : author -> api { label Public; rate 30 req/s; }
flow f_tweet_write : api -> tweets { label Internal; }
flow f_fanout_hot : tweets -> tweets_hot { label Internal; fanout 5; }
flow f_fanout_mean : tweets -> tweets_mean { label Internal; fanout 5; }
flow f_fanout_growth : tweets -> tweets_growth { label Internal; rate 70 req/s; growth 10 %; }

boundary b_ingest endorse f_ingest : foreign -> authenticated when "auth_token_verified"

// UTILIZATION, hottest shard: demand 150/s, single-replica rate 100/s,
// zipf alpha 1.5 over 4 shards concentrates load hard on rank 1 ->
// REFUTED (the same numbers a mean check would call safe).
assert c_hot_shard_utilization bound utilization tweets_hot <= 80 %

// UTILIZATION, mean: same 150/s demand spread over 100/s * 4 replicas =
// 400/s capacity -> 37.5%, PROVED. The averages-lie contrast with
// c_hot_shard_utilization above is the golden.
assert c_mean_shard_utilization bound utilization tweets_mean <= 80 %

// UTILIZATION with growth: utilization0 = 70/100 = 70%, growth 10%/month
// compounding crosses the 80% limit well inside the 24-month horizon ->
// REFUTED with a "saturates in N months (YYYY-MM)" detail, not a bare
// percentage.
assert c_growth_shard_utilization bound utilization tweets_growth <= 80 %
